from docx.oxml.ns import qn

d = Document()
sec=d.sections[0]; sec.top_margin=sec.bottom_margin=Inches(.85); sec.left_margin=sec.right_margin=Inches(.9)
styles=d.styles
styles['Normal'].font.name='Times New Roman'; styles['Normal'].font.size=Pt(11)
styles['Normal'].paragraph_format.line_spacing=1.15
styles['Normal'].paragraph_format.space_after=Pt(7)
for n in ['Title','Heading 1','Heading 2']:
    styles[n].font.name='Times New Roman'; styles[n].font.color.rgb=RGBColor(0,0,0)
styles['Title'].font.size=Pt(20)
styles['Heading 1'].font.size=Pt(14); styles['Heading 2'].font.size=Pt(12)
footer=sec.footer.paragraphs[0]; footer.alignment=WD_ALIGN_PARAGRAPH.CENTER
footer.add_run('Group 31  |  Entrepreneurial Finance  |  ')
field=OxmlElement('w:fldSimple'); field.set(qn('w:instr'),'PAGE'); footer._p.append(field)

def p(s): d.add_paragraph(s)
def h(s,level=1): d.add_heading(s,level)
def table(rows):
    t=d.add_table(rows=0, cols=len(rows[0])); t.alignment=WD_TABLE_ALIGNMENT.CENTER; t.style='Table Grid'
    for i,row in enumerate(rows):
        cells=t.add_row().cells
        for cell,val in zip(cells,row):
            cell.text=val
            for para in cell.paragraphs:
                para.paragraph_format.space_after=Pt(3)
                for run in para.runs:
                    run.font.size=Pt(10)
                    if i==0: run.bold=True
    d.add_paragraph()

t=d.add_paragraph(style='Title'); t.alignment=WD_ALIGN_PARAGRAPH.CENTER; t.add_run('Mobile Banking Financial Inclusion and Cybersecurity Risk in India')
q=d.add_paragraph(); q.alignment=WD_ALIGN_PARAGRAPH.CENTER; q.add_run('Entrepreneurial Finance  |  Term Paper  |  Group 31\nRushal Verma  ·  Vichanshu Raj  ·  Sudhanshu')
h('Abstract')
p('Digital financial services can help people receive payments, save and transact, but greater digital activity also creates opportunities for fraud and makes safe use important. This paper examines the relationship between financial inclusion and digital safety in India using published World Bank Global Findex 2025 indicators, based on surveys conducted in 2024. Account ownership was approximately 89% for both women and men, while the World Bank digital finance dashboard reports digital payment use of 36% among women and 54% among men. The same sources show substantial gaps in access to phones and the internet. These descriptive patterns support a potential market for accessible, trusted security assistance, but they do not establish that digital finance causes scam exposure or that any particular safety practice reduces losses. The India microdata contain 3,000 respondents, but were not available in the supplied folder and no respondent-level statistical tests were performed for this paper. We propose a weighted analysis and an entrepreneurial concept for testing, while distinguishing published evidence from hypotheses.')
p('Keywords: financial inclusion, digital payments, mobile banking, cybersecurity, fraud, entrepreneurship, India')
h('1 Introduction')
p('A financial account can lower the friction of receiving wages, storing savings and paying suppliers. Mobile banking and digital payments extend those services beyond the branch, potentially helping small merchants and individuals transact at lower time and travel cost. However, ownership of an account is only the first stage of inclusion: a person must also be able to access, understand and safely use the service. Fraudulent calls, requests for credentials and misleading payment instructions can undermine confidence and impose losses.')
p('India is a useful setting because account ownership is high while access to devices and use of digital payments remain uneven. This paper asks how inclusion and digital safety fit together, which groups may require additional support, and what business opportunity follows. The focus is the adult population, with relevance to potential entrepreneurs and small merchants; the Findex data do not identify a representative sample of businesses.')
p('The research questions are: (1) How far has access progressed, and how does access differ from active digital use? (2) Are digital finance users more exposed to scams? (3) Are security practices associated with lower vulnerability? (4) Which groups face gaps in adoption and preparedness? Published aggregate data inform the first question and part of the fourth. Questions 2 and 3, and the preparedness part of question 4, require individual-level analysis and are not answered by the results presented here.')
h('2 Background and related evidence')
h('2.1 Inclusion as access and use',2)
p('The World Bank defines account ownership to include accounts at financial institutions or use of a mobile money service. Digital payment measures are narrower than ownership and depend on the exact question: making a digital payment is not necessarily the same as making or receiving one. Phone ownership, smartphone access, connectivity, language, skills and trust can each influence whether a person uses an account digitally. A gender gap in payment use therefore cannot be attributed to cybersecurity alone.')
p('The Global Findex 2025 combines financial inclusion indicators with questions about digital connectivity and safety. Its India survey was conducted in 2024 and the public India catalogue describes 3,000 individual records and 199 variables [1, 2]. The survey is cross-sectional; its 2025 name refers to the database release, not the survey year.')
h('2.2 Digital safety and consumer trust',2)
p('A phone password helps protect access to a device, but it cannot by itself prevent a user from authorizing a deceptive payment. Fraud exposure, sending money to a scammer, financial loss, and perceptions of safety are distinct outcomes. The connectivity questionnaire includes questions on phone PIN or password, receipt of a scam call or text, and sending money to a scammer [2]. A scam call or text is not necessarily connected to banking, and a reported exposure is not proof of a financial loss.')
p('The Reserve Bank of India advises customers not to share passwords, PINs, one-time passwords, CVV or UPI PINs [3]. Practical protection also depends on providers offering understandable warnings, effective reporting and accessible redress. An app that simply teaches users to be careful cannot guarantee security or shift all responsibility for fraud to them.')
h('3 Data and method')
p('This paper is a descriptive secondary-data study. It uses published India figures from the World Bank Global Findex dashboard, gender data portal and India financial and digital inclusion brief [4–6], together with survey documentation [1, 2]. Percentages refer to adults aged 15 and older unless a different denominator is specified. Values are as published; small differences in rounding and indicator definitions matter. No respondent-level dataset was supplied with this workspace, and the paper does not present original weighted estimates, association tests, model coefficients or confidence intervals.')
p('The comparative design separates account ownership, digital payment use, connectivity and safety. Differences are calculated in percentage points by subtracting one published group percentage from another. These comparisons describe different population groups; they do not establish statistical significance or show that the same individuals appear in both indicators. They also do not identify why a gap exists. As a contextual comparison, India’s overall account ownership is compared with the world estimate for the same Findex wave.')
h('4 Findings')
h('4.1 Account access has expanded',2)
p('The World Bank reports that 89.0% of Indian adults owned an account in 2024, compared with 77.5% in 2021 and 53.1% in 2014 [5]. The 2024 world figure was 78.7%. India’s 2024 account ownership was thus 10.3 percentage points above the world figure; the comparison is descriptive and does not measure frequency, quality or safety of account use. The Indian share rose 35.9 percentage points between 2014 and 2024, although the observations do not explain the contribution of any particular policy or technology.')
table([['Indicator','Women','Men','Difference'],['Account ownership','89.2%','88.8%','0.4 pp women higher'],['Digital payment use','36%','54%','18 pp men higher'],['Own a mobile phone','54.3%','78.4%','24.1 pp men higher'],['Own a smartphone','31.8%','52.0%','20.2 pp men higher'],['Internet use in past 30 days','36.3%','55.0%','18.7 pp men higher']])
p('Table 1. India, 2024: selected indicators among adults aged 15+. Account ownership is from [5]; digital payment use from [4]; connectivity from [6]. The payment indicator covers specified digital payment methods; it should not be equated with every broader measure of making or receiving payments. Differences use displayed values and can reflect rounding. These are separate indicators, not a joint distribution.')
h('4.2 Ownership does not imply equal digital use',2)
p('Women and men had almost identical account ownership, yet the dashboard reports an 18 percentage point difference in digital payment use [4]. The connectivity brief reports a 24.1 point gap in mobile phone ownership and an 18.7 point gap in recent internet use [6]. These patterns are consistent with barriers between opening an account and using digital services. They do not prove that a lack of a phone causes the payment gap, because these statistics do not show how the indicators overlap within individuals and do not account for other differences.')
p('Income differences also matter. The dashboard reports account ownership of 86% among the poorest 40% and 91% among the wealthiest 60%, while digital payment use was 43% and 56%, respectively [4]. The displayed gaps are 5 and 13 percentage points. The figures suggest that a product designed only for confident smartphone users could miss a substantial part of the intended market.')
h('4.3 What the available evidence can and cannot say about risk',2)
p('The World Bank report documents device protection and scam exposure internationally [1]. These measures make safety relevant to inclusion, but an international pattern is not an India-specific estimate of financial fraud. The available published comparisons do not establish whether Indian digital payment users receive more scam messages than non-users, whether a password reduces scam losses, or which demographic group has the largest combined adoption and preparedness gap. Such claims need a respondent-level analysis with carefully specified denominators.')
h('5 Proposed respondent level analysis')
p('The following plan implements the group’s progress report when the India file is available. It is a research plan, not a description of work completed. First inspect the latest data dictionary, survey questionnaire, variable labels, coding, weights, eligibility and skip patterns. Confirm the exact construction of anydigpayment, account, con18, con21 and con22 before treating their values as comparable. Codes for refusal and “do not know” must be handled separately from valid no responses. Preserve an audit of exclusions and the unweighted number of observations for every estimate.')
table([['Question','Measure and denominator','Proposed comparison'],['Adoption and inclusion','Account ownership and any digital payment among eligible adults','Weighted shares by gender, age, income, education and location'],['Scam exposure','Reported scam call or text among those asked the question','Weighted exposure by digital payment status, within comparable eligible respondents'],['Safety practice','Phone PIN or password among eligible phone owners','Weighted share by demographic group and payment status'],['Scam response','Sent money to scammer among respondents eligible for the follow-up','Describe weighted share and unweighted count; avoid unstable estimates']])
p('Table 2. Proposed measures. Exact question wording, reference periods and skip logic must be verified in the version of the dataset used. A phone-security measure is a proxy for one aspect of preparedness, not a complete cybersecurity score.')
p('Estimate weighted proportions and differences using the supplied survey weight. Compute confidence intervals and tests using the documented survey design, including strata and primary sampling units when available; do not treat weighted observations as an ordinary simple random sample. If design variables are absent, state the variance-estimation limitation. Compare users and non-users only within the relevant eligibility universe. For adjusted associations, consider a survey-weighted logistic regression of reported scam exposure on digital payment use, age, gender, education, income, employment and urbanicity, subject to sample size and data availability. Report adjusted predictions with uncertainty and clearly distinguish the exposure outcome from actual loss.')
p('To examine protective practices, compare responses among eligible phone owners, taking care that con22 may be asked only after an exposure and may have few positive cases. A cross-sectional comparison cannot determine whether a safety practice preceded an incident; reverse causation, unobserved digital activity, recall error and selection into phone ownership all remain possible. A low count of respondents sending money should preclude elaborate models. RQ1 is partly definitional: account use is one dimension of inclusion, so an association between a payment measure and an inclusion index containing that same measure would be circular.')
h('6 Entrepreneurial opportunity')
h('6.1 Proposed service',2)
p('The evidence suggests a testable opportunity for a bank or fintech partner to offer a multilingual digital payment safety companion. The proposed service would explain common scam patterns in short audio and visual lessons, use a simple checklist before a user approves a payment, and provide an easy route to the bank’s verified support and fraud-reporting channels. It should work on low-cost devices and be tested with first-time users, including women and small merchants. This is a concept based on observed access and usage gaps, not evidence of proven demand or effectiveness.')
p('The first customer could be a bank, payment provider or merchant network that pays for onboarding and support; end users could receive the service without an additional fee. A provider partnership could supply verified support links, though willingness to pay and access to distribution remain untested. The service should never request an OTP, UPI PIN, bank password or remote access. Because warnings can be ignored or misunderstood, the provider should retain clear responsibility for its own fraud controls and complaint handling.')
h('6.2 Validation and financial logic',2)
p('Interview prospective users and partners before building: test whether they recognize a genuine payment request, where they seek help, and what prevents use of existing channels. Then run a small pilot with informed consent and a comparison group if feasible. Track lesson completion, comprehension on unseen scenarios, the proportion finding the correct reporting channel, false alarms, accessibility and support costs. Longer-term outcomes could include verified fraud reports or losses, but only with adequate sample size, consistent observation periods and privacy safeguards. A rise in reports could reflect improved reporting rather than more fraud.')
p('Unit economics require evidence: contribution per institutional customer equals subscription revenue minus onboarding, support, content and infrastructure costs. The service becomes viable only if recurring revenue covers fixed development and compliance costs at a plausible adoption scale. No market size, price, cost or return is asserted here because none has been validated. An independent evaluation should also check whether warnings discourage legitimate transactions or exclude users who rely on assistance.')
h('7 Limitations and future research')
p('This paper relies on published aggregates and reports no original microdata results. Findex is self-reported, cross-sectional and covers adults, not firms specifically. Access, payment and connectivity estimates may have different wording and eligibility rules. Group differences cannot isolate cybersecurity concerns from income, education, device access, social norms or other factors. The comparison with the world is a population aggregate rather than a peer-country analysis; it should not be interpreted as a ranking of system quality.')
p('Future work should acquire the India microdata under the World Bank’s terms, reproduce weighted published estimates where definitions match, and complete the planned group comparisons. Linking self-reported exposure to independently verified losses would require additional data and appropriate consent. A randomized evaluation of safety interventions could help identify effects, provided the study protects users and measures unintended consequences.')
h('8 Conclusion')
p('India’s 2024 Findex figures show widespread account ownership alongside uneven digital payment use and device access. The gaps indicate that financial inclusion should be assessed beyond whether an account exists. A low-friction safety and support service is a plausible entrepreneurial idea, but its demand, effect on fraud and financial viability remain to be tested. The proposed weighted microdata analysis is necessary before making claims about the relationship between digital-finance use and scam exposure in India.')
h('References')
refs=[
'[1] World Bank. The Global Findex Database 2025: Connectivity and Financial Inclusion in the Digital Economy. https://www.worldbank.org/en/publication/globalfindex/report',
'[2] World Bank. India: The Global Findex Database 2025, 2024 survey, IND_2024_FINDEX_v02_M. Data dictionary and study documentation. https://doi.org/10.48529/c9vy-pq51',
'[3] Reserve Bank of India. Safeguards for Digital Banking. https://rbikehtahai.rbi.org.in/safeguards-for-digital-banking.html',
'[4] World Bank. India: Global snapshot of indicators and enabling regulations, 2024 Findex indicators. https://digitalfinance.worldbank.org/country/india',
'[5] World Bank Gender Data Portal. Account ownership at a financial institution or with a mobile-money-service provider. https://genderdata.worldbank.org/en/indicator/fx-own-totl-zs?geos=WLD_IND&view=trend',
'[6] World Bank. India Financial and Digital Inclusion, Gender Data Landscape, Fall 2025. https://documents1.worldbank.org/curated/en/099116007012231127/pdf/IDU098b40b9e0f0b6043ec0b51d07c00e31478db.pdf',
'[7] World Bank. Global Findex download data and methodology. https://www.worldbank.org/en/publication/globalfindex/download-data']
for r in refs: p(r)
